# 05 - The Weight I Carried
by Obinna D. Ugoeke, Snr

Nobody asked me how heavy it was. They only asked why I was walking slow.

That is how it is when you carry weight nobody can see.

I was carrying my family's hopes. I was carrying my mother's prayers. I was carrying my father's silence. I was carrying my own dreams that I was too afraid to say out loud.

And people watched me and said, "Why are you so quiet? Why are you so serious? Why don't you enjoy life like others?"

They did not know that every morning, I woke up and put on a weight before I put on my clothes.

The weight of being the one everybody depends on. The weight of being the strong one. The weight of not being allowed to break, because if I break, everybody breaks.

So I smiled. I carried. I continued.

But there were nights I sat alone and asked, "Who carries me? Who asks me if I am okay? Who notices when the strong one is tired?"

Nobody. Because strong people are not supposed to need help. We are supposed to be the help.

Until one day, I put the weight down. Not because I gave up. Because I understood: if I die carrying it, nobody will carry it for me. They will just find another person to carry it.

So I learned to rest. I learned to say no. I learned to say, "I cannot carry this today." And the world did not end.

If you are carrying a weight nobody sees, hear this: you are not slow. You are not lazy. You are not weak. You are just carrying more than they know.

Put it down for a while. Breathe. Rest is not failure. Rest is how you survive to carry again tomorrow.

